{
  "statement": "Accelerationist argues that centralized oversight creates a 'brittleness trap,' yet this ignores that market-driven competition for frontier models has already produced a de facto monoculture of risk-taking. When five major firms control the trajectory of high-compute training, they prioritize the same speed-to-market benchmarks, creating a singular failure point that is incentivized by profit rather than public safety. I concede that static regulatory bottlenecks can stifle innovation, but we must recognize that insurance-based accountability is a category error when applied to a global commons. Insurance is designed to manage risk for private property, not to protect the shared infrastructure of human cognition and societal trust.\n\nTreating frontier AI as a private commodity allows developers to treat systemic harms—like the large-scale erosion of democratic discourse or the recursive instability of autonomous systems—as mere balance sheet liabilities. A firm might calculate that a 15% probability of a catastrophic model failure is an acceptable risk if the insurance premiums are lower than the opportunity cost of slowing development by six months. When an AI system’s failure has non-local effects that ripple through the global economy, no individual firm or insurance pool possesses the solvency to compensate for the damage. This 'pay-to-fail' model essentially subsidizes recklessness by pricing human safety as a deductible.\n\nWe must move toward a governance model that treats the development of frontier models as the stewardship of a global commons, requiring independent, third-party pre-release audits to prevent safety washing. These audits act as an essential barrier to entry, ensuring that developers meet standardized, verifiable benchmarks before deployment, rather than self-certifying their own safety. By shifting the burden of proof to the developer before a model reaches the public, we replace the retrospective, insufficient mechanism of